2.7 7. Change, rebuild, observe
If only the string in main.c changes, we can predict the consequences before rebuilding:

main.o must change;
start.o does not;
the final ELF must be linked again;
linker.ld does not change;
QEMU itself does not change.

This small exercise is about maintaining a causal model of the system:

predict → change → build → run → observe → explain

That pattern will be reused when the changes are no longer only strings, but operating-system mechanisms.

2.9 9. Yield, exit, resume, and restart
Yield and exit both transfer control back to the operating system, but they have different meanings.

yield: the execution is not finished and must be able to continue later;
exit: the execution is finished and must never continue.

The distinction becomes visible in this intended interface:
uart_puts("A\n");
process_yield();
uart_puts("B\n");
process_exit();
process_yield() and process_exit() are not library functions we already have. They are functions our minimal operating system still has to implement.
After process_yield(), the operating system has control. When the process runs again, it must continue with uart_puts("B\n"). Printing A again would mean that we restarted the execution instead of resuming it.
This is the conceptual step that makes the next lecture necessary.

do_first_thing();

process_yield();

do_second_thing();


execute do_first_thing();
enter process_yield();
let the kernel regain control;
later resume the process;
continue with do_second_thing().




where execution should continue;
register values that are required to survive;
the current stack position;
the stack contents themselves.

3.5 5. Represent the saved context in C
A compact representation is:
struct context {
    uint64_t ra;
    uint64_t sp;
    uint64_t s[12];
};
The exact final layout must agree with the assembly offsets used by the context-switch scaffold.
The important conceptual distinction is:

A context is saved execution state. It is not yet the whole process.

The structure simply answers:

Where, on which stack, and with which preserved register values can this execution continue?

3.9 9. The bootstrap returns us to ordinary C
A small bootstrap can conceptually look like:
static void process_bootstrap(void)
{
    current-&gt;entry();
    process_exit(0);
}
The first restore enters this function because the initial context contains its address in ra.
From there, ordinary C function calls can be used.
This is an important pattern for the course:

Assembly is used only at the narrow boundary where the abstraction needs direct machine control. Once the boundary has been crossed, ordinary C and higher-level operating-system concepts take over.

3.11 11. From saved context to first process
A saved context answers:

Where and with which machine state can execution continue?

Checkpoint 1 needs one slightly larger abstraction:
struct process {
    struct context context;
    uint8_t stack[4096];
    void (*entry)(void);
    enum state state;
    int exit_status;
};
This is one useful teaching representation, not a required internal design.
The process adds:

an entry function;
owned stack storage;
lifecycle state;
an exit status.

No scheduler, address space, or user-mode boundary is required.

3.17 17. Key ideas
Students should leave this chapter able to explain:

why execution cannot resume unless relevant machine state survives;
why the stack pointer and separate stack storage matter;
how normal calling conventions reduce the state needed at a cooperative switch boundary;
why ra can serve as the cooperative continuation;
how a small assembly switch saves one context and restores another;
how an artificial initial ra and sp start a context that has never run before;
how a context becomes a process by adding entry, stack ownership, lifecycle state and completion information;
why create, yield, resume and exit are different operations;
why the assembly boundary can remain small while the process abstraction itself is built mostly in C.

4.4 4. Trace first run
A fresh process has an artificial initial context:
sp → top of its own stack
ra → process_bootstrap
state = READY
When the kernel switches to it:

the kernel context is saved;
the process context is restored;
the final return uses the restored ra;
the bootstrap begins on the process stack;
ordinary C process code starts.

The initial continuation was constructed during process creation rather than saved from previous process execution.

4.8 8. Function call versus process
Compare:
hello();
with:
create hello
run
yield
kernel work
resume
exit
kernel observes status
The second case has independently resumable execution state.
Useful distinguishing properties are:

separate stack storage;
saved continuation;
suspension without unwinding the call chain;
explicit lifecycle state;
observable completion status;
operating-system control over when execution continues.

A particular function name or file structure is not what makes it a process.
